1. Primary Key
.unique column/field in a table 
.should not contain a duplicate or NULL value 
.identifies each record in a table uniquely 
.helps establish relationships between different tables 
.maintains the integrity of DB by preventing duplicate and null values in the key column 
.can only be one in a table but can be defined on one or more fields - composite primary key 
.can use the AUTO_INCREMENT attribute on this field to automatically add a new value to the column when 
    a new row is created
.makes query execution fast - mysql creates a primary index for the primary key 

>>rules:
    .each value must be unique 
    .no NULL values 
    .primary key value should remain constant throughout the lifespan of the record 
    .should either be system-generated e.g auto-incremented integers OR 
        natural keys e.g existing data that uniquely identifies a record 

>creating PK
.either:
    .create table statement 
    .alter table statement 
.drop PK using alter table statement 




2. Foreign Key 
.field/column in a table which refers to a PK in another table 
.also called referencing key 
    >it creates a link/relation between two tables thus creating referential integrity 
.it creates a relationship between the columns in the current table (one with FK) and the referenced table 
    (one with PK)
.creates a parent-child type of relationship where:
    .the table with the FK in the child table refers to the primary/unique key column in the parent table 

>creating FK 
    .create table 
    .alter table 

>> child table - table where the FK is defined 
   child id - PK column of the child table 
   parent id - FK column in the child table that refers to the PK in the parent table 

        create table FK (
    -> user_id int primary key,
    -> username varchar(20),
    -> email varchar(30) unique not null
    -> );

    create table FK2 (
    -> order_id int primary key,
    -> user_id int,
    -> order_date date,
    -> total_amount decimal(10, 2),
    -> foreign key (user_id) references FK(user_id)
    -> );



